{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450604-v20-need-to-brainstorm-etiquette-challenges-in-the-victorian-era","id":1450604,"title":"[V20] Need to Brainstorm Etiquette Challenges in the Victorian Era","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-01T10:30:48-07:00","id":1450604,"title":"[V20] Need to Brainstorm Etiquette Challenges in the Victorian Era","content":"I'm trying to set up situations where PCs will actually use the skills that players have invested points in. I'm running an ancillae game in Victorian Age Hong Kong, and, after Politics (because we discussed that people wanted a political game, alongside some Masquerade fixing), the ability characters have sunk the most points into is Etiquette.<br /> <br /> What challenges would you give characters for Etiquette in this setting? Passing of social mishaps as something else in Elysium, knowing how to conduct oneself in mortal society as necessary,, and the like are all fine, but I'm a little unsure what else to do with this skill.<br /> <br /> Any ideas would be greatly appreciated!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://wondrousatlas.wordpress.com\" target=\"_blank\">Wondrous Atlas of Creation's Destiny</a> - I cohost an Exalted podcast focusing on examining the setting's lore and how to develop them into elements to use in games.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/924488-homebuilt-exalted-st-screen-reference-sheet\" target=\"_blank\">Ex3 Reference Materials</a> - currently includes an ST screen, common actions sheet, weapons reference sheet, character creation summary and mortal QCs reference sheet.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/497-xerxes","name":"Xerxes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar497_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"557"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-01T11:39:26-07:00","id":1450617,"title":"","content":"Seduction used to be ettiquette. As did grabbing people's attention for eye-powers like Dominate. <br /> -knowing how to haggle. <br /> dressing the part. Fashion before the later part of last century was all about fitting in, not standing out. you can be very meticulous about clothing if thats something enjoyable for you. The same kinda goes for manners. These seemingly bizzare rituals exist to seperate the pounds from the pennies after all. <br /> <br /> Not insulting locals. <br /> Understanding euphemisms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-02T12:53:16-07:00","id":1450723,"title":"","content":"Etiquette is a weird skill because it can be defined in a lot of ways.<br /> <br /> Besides &quot;proper behavior&quot;, it is also one of those skills that allows people to get what they want so it can used in various negotiations, seductions, or sales. <br /> <br /> It also prevents you from saying the wrong thing at the wrong time. If you aren't suppose to conduct business until a certain part of the evening, then you know to wait until then to broach any subject.<br /> <br /> It also seems to include any knowledge or skill that may be required to fit into society. So if it is something that may come up in a social situation, a person can use Etiquette to be able to do that. Dancing done at social functions, especially any kind of formal dance, is covered by Etiquette. Popular songs sung at social gatherings for mutual entertainment would be covered, as would parlor games. Normally these would be seen as belonging to another Ability, but in terms of knowing how to act socially - as opposed to truly giving a professional performance - Etiquette would cover it. Anything a person might feel that they could be at a loss at in terms of how to handle, Etiquette provides them the knowledge of what to do so they fit in and can participate.<br /> <br /> Etiquette in games often means &quot;this is how you act in posh society&quot;. So it is very specific knowledge of a certain class. That contradicts with the idea that it is also how you get things done in negotiation, etc. So I have heard some STs use it less as specifically to posh society, but &quot;you know how to act in any kind of society&quot; that includes lower class, criminal class, etc. It allows you to navigate through any kind of sub-culture. You may not want to use it that way and determine other Abilities that should be used for that at different social classes, but it is an option.<br /> <br /> If you are looking for general ideas, you may want to watch the first season of Downtown Abbey. It deals with a professional middle class lawyer and his mother integrating into high society once he becomes the heir of a title and estate. There are lots of social mistakes he makes as he learns to fit in and accept the new social requirements of his position. That might give you some neat little scenes you can include.<br /> <br /> In terms of actual challenges and importance of the skill, I think there's lots of ways you could make it more prominent.<br /> <br /> There's a particular elder the PCs need to impress. Maybe he's a regular NPC in your chronicle that you designated for this in any kind of interaction, or an NPC elder visiting from another domain that they have to interact with. He's very particular so it is always several Etiquette rolls during their interactions besides whatever it is they need to do. Any failure increases difficulty of their success or causes automatic failure. In this case, it is very important that the NPC never need to approach them to help. The PCs should always need to go to that NPC.<br /> <br /> PCs need to interact with some other group that is alienated from their normal human or sect society. The PCs therefore need to establish that they are a group, representatives, supplicants, etc. that this other group can do business with despite mistrust or hostility. Maybe it is an Independent Clan. Maybe they have to meet with some faction of Lupines (like Glass Walkers) to maintain the peace or smooth over a misunderstanding or ask for a favor or something, or some other supernatural group like a society of sorcerers that know you are a vampire. Maybe its as your mortal persona dealing with bureaucrats, unions, businessmen, or some other group they are not a part of. They need to get them to do something. In all cases, the initial contact is a several etiquette roles that simply serve to establish trust and that you know how to properly act towards them on their turf, that they &quot;understand them&quot;. Then you can do other rolls in future meetings, but maybe always require an initial Etiquette roll up front.<br /> <br /> Another thing is that during whatever social event, you may ask for an Etiquette roll whenever the scene of the event changes. Make one roll when you arrive. Another when the ball dancing begins. A third when the party breaks up into smaller conversations. Maybe they need a number of successes all together, or at least one success at each event, in order to be invited to the next social event, gain entry into some coterie, or impress the Primogen. <br /> <br /> Maybe it's just as something simple like being able to succeed at a Etiquette roll so a lady is willing to dance with you (because you know how to do that dance), in order to cultivate her for future feeding, groom her to be part of your herd; expand your social circle; be able to be introduced to her family; gain points with the Toreador, or whatever.<br /> <br /> Since this is Victorian Age Hong Kong, you are also dealing with racial and cultural issues here. How one acts in European society, Chinese society, or when the two mix could be crucial (not to mention when interacting with people of different social classes within each race). Someone who is able to navigate through all of them could gain important advantages by being the first to know things and react appropriately. For some ideas, you may want to read Tai-Pan by James Clavell which takes place in the 1840s during the founding of Hong Kong and deals with a lot of these issues.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}